  "voting_summary": "Washington is an all-mail state: every vote arrives by mail or drop box, and ballots postmarked by election day are accepted until certification, nearly three weeks later. Polls close at 11 p.m. Eastern; counties release a large pre-processed dump shortly after closing, then a batch each afternoon for one to two weeks as late mail verifies. Later batches lean Democratic (election-day-postmarked ballots skew younger and bluer), so close races tighten leftward over the week after election night.",
